INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'us-job-cuts-october-2026-ai-153074-20261103', 'global', 'USA',
'US Job Cuts Hit 153,074 in October 2026 as AI Restructuring Reaches Record Level',
'',
'United States employers announced 153,074 job cuts in October 2026, the highest October total since 2003, with cost-cutting at 50,437 and AI-attributed restructuring at 31,039, according to Challenger, Gray and Christmas.',
'',
$$<p>On 3 November 2026, a <a href="https://startupfortune.com/us-employers-announced-153074-job-cuts-in-october-the-most-since-2003/">new report on the United States labour market</a> put a stark number on a year of restructuring: US job cuts in October 2026 reached 153,074, the highest total for any October since 2003, according to outplacement firm Challenger, Gray &amp; Christmas. The US job cuts October 2026 figure is up 175 per cent from the 55,597 cuts announced in October of the previous year, and the firm said artificial intelligence was the second-most cited reason for the cuts, behind cost-cutting. For jobseekers, the report is both a warning and a map: it shows where announced cuts are concentrated, which reasons employers are actually giving, and why the headlines about AI and jobs need reading with care.</p>
<p>Two cautions belong at the start. First, Challenger counts announced job cuts, not confirmed redundancies on a given date: an announcement is an employer's stated plan, and plans change, shrink or stretch over time. Second, a reason cited in a filing is the employer's stated reason, not proof of the underlying cause. When an employer attributes cuts to AI, it is saying that software and automation are taking over work a person used to do; when it cites cost-cutting, it is describing belt-tightening. Both categories can overlap in practice, and the report keeps them separate, so readers should not add them together or treat one as a confession about the other. With those cautions in mind, the numbers themselves are striking.</p>
<h2>US job cuts October 2026: at a glance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Source</th><td>Challenger, Gray &amp; Christmas, October job cuts report, as reported 3 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total announced cuts in October 2026</th><td>153,074</td></tr>
<tr><th>Change from October last year</th><td>Up 175 per cent, from 55,597</td></tr>
<tr><th>Change from September 2026</th><td>Up 183 per cent month on month</td></tr>
<tr><th>Highest October total since</th><td>2003</td></tr>
<tr><th>Leading reason</th><td>Cost-cutting, 50,437 cuts</td></tr>
<tr><th>AI-attributed cuts</th><td>31,039, the largest AI-attributed monthly total of 2026 so far</td></tr>
<tr><th>Leading sector</th><td>Warehousing, 47,878 cuts</td></tr>
<tr><th>Technology sector cuts</th><td>33,281, nearly six times the September total</td></tr>
<tr><th>Year-to-date cuts in 2026</th><td>1,099,500, already 44 per cent above the full-year 2024 total</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Where the cuts fell, and what AI had to do with them</h2>
<p>Warehousing led all sectors in October with 47,878 announced cuts, but technology was close behind and drove the AI narrative, shedding 33,281 jobs in a single month. That technology figure is nearly six times the sector's September total, and it pushed technology's cumulative toll for 2026 to roughly 141,000 cuts, already above the previous full-year total. The pattern fits a wider wave of technology workforce reductions reported in the same week: software companies have been restructuring as automated tools take on more of the work, and some have said so directly while others have described the same restructuring in the language of simplification and efficiency. The Challenger data adds a measurable anchor to that wave, because it records what employers themselves cited, sector by sector, rather than relying on rumour.</p>
<p>Cost-cutting still topped the list of stated reasons at 50,437 cuts, but AI-related restructuring came close behind at 31,039, the largest chunk of AI-attributed layoffs the firm has recorded in any single month this year. That distinction is important. Earlier in 2026, AI had already become a leading cited reason for cuts: Challenger's own archive notes that AI led all reasons for a fifth consecutive month in July 2026, and separate reporting in October put <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-jobs-india-hiring-october-naukri-jobspeak-20261103">AI-linked cuts</a> through September 2026 at more than 120,000. The October figure extends that trend rather than starting it. At the same time, the firm's analysts have pointed to a mix of forces behind the October surge, including AI adoption, softening consumer and corporate spending, rising costs, and a correction after the hiring boom of the pandemic years. No single cause explains 153,074 announcements.</p>
<h2>What this means for jobseekers, in practical terms</h2>
<p>For workers in technology and warehousing, the immediate implication is competition: more experienced candidates are entering the market at once, and employers that are still hiring can afford to be selective. The useful response is not panic but targeting. Candidates should aim at employers and functions where hiring is still expanding rather than at the sectors announcing the largest cuts, and they should be ready to show, in concrete terms, what they can do that automation cannot yet do reliably: judgement under uncertainty, work with messy real-world data, customer and operational problems that do not fit a template, and delivery across teams. For early-career workers in AI-exposed fields, the pressure is sharper, because employers are testing automated tools first on routine, entry-level work; building a portfolio of completed work, rather than relying on a qualification alone, is the strongest answer available.</p>
<p>For readers outside the United States, including in Nepal, the report carries a different lesson. These are announced cuts by United States employers, and they do not directly change visa rules or overseas hiring channels; no legitimate employer or agent can convert a layoff report into a job offer, and anyone charging a fee on the back of this news should be treated as a scam. What the report does change is the global conversation: when the world's largest labour market records its worst October for cuts in 22 years, with AI named directly in more than 31,000 of them, employers everywhere start asking the same questions about which roles automation reshapes. Workers who can work alongside AI tools, rather than compete with them on routine tasks, are better placed wherever they apply. The year-to-date total of 1,099,500 announced cuts is already the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/singapore-q3-2026-retrenchments-4800-20261030">highest since the pandemic period</a>, and that scale, more than any single company's announcement, is the signal jobseekers should take seriously.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Startup Fortune report on the Challenger, Gray &amp; Christmas October 2026 figures, published 3 November 2026; Challenger, Gray &amp; Christmas report archive summary of the October figures.</small></p>
$$,
